analysis.originalLogic:
MIR validation's `TypeChecker` handled `CastKind::PointerCoercion(PointerCoercion::Unsize, _)` with only a comment and no actual check:

```rust
CastKind::PointerCoercion(PointerCoercion::Unsize, _) => {
    // This is used for all `CoerceUnsized` types,
    // not just pointers/references, so is hard to check.
}
```

Separately, codegen assumed that a same-principal-def-id trait-object upcast was a no-op ("A NOP cast that doesn't actually change anything, should be allowed even with invalid vtables."), without asserting the principal trait refs were actually equal. The PR adds a `predicate_must_hold_modulo_regions` helper in `compiler/rustc_mir_transform/src/validate.rs` and requires the `CoerceUnsized` bound on unsize casts.
